WEBVTT - Most Fascinating Stories - Episode 107: Osceola and the Seminole Resistance: The Unconquered Everglades

1
00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:05.920
[Rebecca] Welcome back to Most Fascinating Stories.

2
00:00:05.920 --> 00:00:07.200
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

3
00:00:07.200 --> 00:00:22.080
[Rebecca] Today, we journey into the primeval sawgrass wilderness of the Florida Everglades during the 1830s to witness the longest, costliest, and deadliest indigenous conflict in United States history: the Second Seminole War.

4
00:00:22.080 --> 00:00:35.760
[Rebecca] Under President Andrew Jackson's Indian Removal Act, the federal government resolved to forcefully deport the Seminole people from their ancestral Florida homelands to dry reservations in modern-day Oklahoma.

5
00:00:35.760 --> 00:00:39.600
[Rebecca] But the Seminoles refused to surrender their sacred soil.

6
00:00:39.600 --> 00:00:47.200
[Rebecca] Led by a charismatic, fearless 30-year-old war chief named Osceola, Seminole warriors and allied escaped black slaves.

7
00:00:47.200 --> 00:00:55.520
[Rebecca] The Black Seminoles retreated into the impenetrable labyrinth of Cypress swamps, defeating three commanding generals of the United States Army.

8
00:00:55.520 --> 00:01:04.080
[Rebecca] Captured only through an infamous act of military treachery under a white flag of truce, Osceola became an enduring global legend of defiance.

9
00:01:04.080 --> 00:01:09.840
[Rebecca] Here is George with the full, gripping story.

10
00:01:46.080 --> 00:01:59.920
[George] Deepening this cultural alliance was the presence of hundreds of escaped African American slaves who had fled brutal plantations in Georgia, the Carolinas, and Alabama over decades, finding sanctuary among the Seminole clans.

11
00:02:00.360 --> 00:02:14.760
[George] Known as the Black Seminoles, these African Americans established prosperous agricultural villages alongside Seminole settlements, adopting indigenous dress and customs while serving as interpreters, advisers, and elite warriors.

12
00:02:14.760 --> 00:02:25.240
[George] Because Southern plantation owners were terrified of an armed, free black haven on their southern border, Florida became a prime target for American military expansion.

13
00:02:25.240 --> 00:02:37.240
[George] Following the acquisition of Florida from Spain in 1821 and the ascension of President Andrew Jackson in 1829, the federal government enacted the Indian Removal Act of 1830.

14
00:02:37.240 --> 00:02:44.680
[George] Jackson's administration demanded that all Eastern indigenous tribes be rounded up and deported west of the Mississippi River.

15
00:02:44.680 --> 00:02:56.520
[George] At the Treaty of Paine's Landing in 1832, federal commissioners used bribery, coercion, and liquor to induce several elderly Seminole chiefs to sign a fraudulent removal treaty.

16
00:02:56.520 --> 00:03:03.720
[George] But a new generation of young warriors was rising who vowed that they would die in the swamps before surrendering an inch of Florida.

17
00:03:03.720 --> 00:03:15.240
[George] The subtropical wilderness of Florida provided an impenetrable defensive fortress of blackwater cypress sloughs, sawgrass prairies, and hidden hammock islands unknown to white settlers.

18
00:03:15.240 --> 00:03:23.240
[George] Leading this fierce opposition was a young, 30-year-old war leader named Osceola, known in his youth as Billy Powell.

19
00:03:23.240 --> 00:03:40.200
[George] Born around 1804 in Alabama of mixed Muscogee Creek and Scottish descent, Osceola possessed piercing dark eyes, an athletic build, and a mesmerizing oratorical presence that commanded absolute respect among both Seminole warriors and black Seminole leaders.

20
00:03:40.200 --> 00:03:56.080
[George] In early 1835, at Fort King near modern-day Ocala, Federal Indian agent Wiley Thompson convened a council of Seminole leaders, demanding they sign a final agreement confirming the Payne's Landing Treaty and prepare for immediate deportation.

21
00:03:56.080 --> 00:04:02.560
[George] While several intimidated chiefs affixed their marks, Ossiola strode to the council table in fury.

22
00:04:02.560 --> 00:04:12.080
[George] Reaching into his belt, he drew his hunting knife, slammed the blade violently through the parchment treaty into the oak table, and declared with ringing defiance.

23
00:04:12.080 --> 00:04:16.000
[George] The only treaty I will ever sign with the white man is with this.

24
00:04:16.000 --> 00:04:25.120
[George] Enraged by this public insolence, Agent Thompson had Ossiola seized, shackled in heavy iron irons, and thrown into the Fort Guardhouse.

25
00:04:25.120 --> 00:04:32.640
[George] For several days, Ossiola languished in confinement, pretending to submit and feigning contrition to secure his release.

26
00:04:32.640 --> 00:04:36.800
[George] But the humiliation of the iron shackles burned deep into his soul.

27
00:04:36.800 --> 00:04:46.320
[George] Once released, Ossiola slipped into the wilderness, sending warrunners through every swamp village to prepare for total, uncompromising resistance.

28
00:04:46.320 --> 00:04:57.680
[George] Ossiola's sharp hunting knife impaled the fraudulent removal parchment deep into the wooden table, sending a clear, unequivocal message that Florida's indigenous soil was not for sale.

29
00:04:57.680 --> 00:05:07.440
[George] On December 28, 1835, Ossiola orchestrated a simultaneous, devastating double blow that ignited the Second Seminole War.

30
00:05:07.440 --> 00:05:18.640
[George] That morning, Major Francis Langhorn Dade was marching a relief column of 108 United States Army regulars along the military road from Fort Brook in Tampa to Fort King.

31
00:05:18.640 --> 00:05:32.360
[George] As the soldiers passed through a stand of pine flatwoods near modern-day Bushnell, over 200 Seminole and Black Seminole warriors under Chiefs McInopy, Jumper, and Alligator ambushed the column from behind palmetto bushes.

32
00:05:32.360 --> 00:05:42.440
[George] In a matter of hours, Dade and 105 of his soldiers were killed, only three wounded soldiers survived by feigning death beneath the bodies of comrades.

33
00:05:42.440 --> 00:05:51.080
[George] It was the worst military defeat suffered by the United States Army against an indigenous force prior to Custer's last stand forty years later.

34
00:05:51.080 --> 00:05:57.960
[George] At that very same hour, 50 miles to the north, Ossiola carried out his personal vengeance at Fort King.

35
00:05:57.960 --> 00:06:10.520
[George] Concealed in the woods outside the garrison, Ossiola and a small war party ambushed Indian agent Wiley Thompson and Lieutenant Constantine Smith as they strolled outside the Fort Palisade after lunch.

36
00:06:10.520 --> 00:06:17.480
[George] Thompson was shot fourteen times, fulfilling Ossiola's vow to punish the man who had placed him in chains.

37
00:06:17.480 --> 00:06:24.920
[George] The war had begun in earnest, and the United States government found itself plunged into an intractable military quagmire.

38
00:06:24.920 --> 00:06:36.280
[George] The stunning ambush of Major Dade's command sent shockwaves through Washington, demonstrating that Seminole warriors possessed superior tactical discipline and marksmanship in native terrain.

39
00:06:36.280 --> 00:06:56.520
[George] For two grueling years, the United States Army deployed over 40,000 regular soldiers, state militia, and naval marines to crush the Seminole resistance, commanded successively by four of the most renowned generals in the military, Duncan Clinch, Winfield Scott, Richard Keith Call, and Thomas Jessup.

40
00:06:56.520 --> 00:07:01.880
[George] Yet every conventional military campaign ended in humiliating failure and exhaustion.

41
00:07:01.880 --> 00:07:10.440
[George] Osciola and his war chiefs understood that they could not defeat the United States Army in open, stand-up European-style artillery battles.

42
00:07:10.440 --> 00:07:17.520
[George] Instead, they waged a brilliant, asymmetrical guerrilla war perfectly adapted to the unforgiving Florida terrain.

43
00:07:17.840 --> 00:07:29.680
[George] Seminole warriors retreated deep into the sawgrass expanses of the Everglades, moving families and livestock to secluded tree islands known as hammocks, completely invisible from dry land.

44
00:07:29.680 --> 00:07:39.360
[George] American soldiers clad in heavy wool uniforms, carrying heavy muskets and baggage wagons, were hopelessly bogged down in waste deep black muck.

45
00:07:39.360 --> 00:07:51.200
[George] Swarms of mosquitoes, venomous water moccasins, alligators and rampant outbreaks of malaria, yellow fever and dysentery decimated the ranks far more effectively than bullets.

46
00:07:51.200 --> 00:08:00.560
[George] Whenever military columns advanced, Seminole snipers fired from hidden mangrove blinds and vanished into the fog before the army could deploy artillery.

47
00:08:00.560 --> 00:08:10.000
[George] Supply lines were cut, military outposts were burned, and millions of dollars in federal war appropriations evaporated in the Florida swamps.

48
00:08:10.000 --> 00:08:21.200
[George] Army officers and soldiers suffered miserably from unrelenting swarms of mosquitoes, venomous snakes, and debilitating fevers that incapacitated entire regiments within weeks.

49
00:08:21.200 --> 00:08:33.760
[George] Frustrated by his inability to capture Osceola on the battlefield and facing intense political pressure from Washington, Major General Thomas Jessup resolved to discard the honorable rules of warfare.

50
00:08:33.760 --> 00:08:47.120
[George] In October 1837, Osceola, suffering from severe chronic malaria and exhausted by constant combat, agreed to meet federal officers for peace negotiations near Fort Payton, just south of St.

51
00:08:47.120 --> 00:08:48.240
[George] Augustine.

52
00:08:48.240 --> 00:08:59.440
[George] On October 21, 1837, Osceola and 80 of his warriors and chiefs approached the designated meeting ground under an official, recognized white flag of truce.

53
00:08:59.440 --> 00:09:08.360
[George] They carried no weapons, assuming that the sacred international protocol of the truce flag would be respected by civilized military officers.

54
00:09:08.360 --> 00:09:11.240
[George] Waiting at the site was Brigadier General Joseph M.

55
00:09:11.240 --> 00:09:14.200
[George] Hernandez and two hundred armed cavalrymen.

56
00:09:14.200 --> 00:09:20.920
[George] As Osceola stepped forward under the white flag, General Hernandez suddenly gave a prearranged signal.

57
00:09:20.920 --> 00:09:28.600
[George] Cavalry troops surrounded the unarmed Seminoles with drawn carbines, disarming and binding them before Osciola could react.

58
00:09:28.600 --> 00:09:33.560
[George] General Jessup had ordered Osciola taken alive through deliberate deceit.

59
00:09:33.560 --> 00:09:49.320
[George] It was one of the most disgraceful and cowardly betrayals in American military history, an act that shocked public opinion in New York, Philadelphia, and London, with leading newspapers denouncing Jessup for trampling upon the sacred honor of the United States flag.

60
00:09:49.320 --> 00:10:00.840
[George] General Jessup's deceitful violation of the white flag of truce drew blistering condemnation from members of Congress, who denounced the capture as a stain upon the nation's military honor.

61
00:10:00.840 --> 00:10:04.760
[George] Bound in chains, Osceola was paraded through the streets of St.

62
00:10:04.760 --> 00:10:13.240
[George] Augustine and imprisoned inside the stone dungeons of the historic Spanish fortress Castillo de San Marcos, then known as Fort Marion.

63
00:10:13.240 --> 00:10:23.960
[George] Despite his confinement, Osciola carried himself with extraordinary poise and dignity, captivating the local population and visitors who came to view the famous war chief.

64
00:10:23.960 --> 00:10:27.880
[George] Celebrated frontier painter George Catlin traveled to St.

65
00:10:27.880 --> 00:10:36.920
[George] Augustine to paint Osciola's portrait, describing the chief as a man of extraordinary intellect, graceful manners, and magnetic presence.

66
00:10:36.920 --> 00:10:52.960
[George] Catlin created two iconic oil portraits depicting Osciola dressed in traditional Seminole regalia, a vibrant calico hunting shirt, a beaded bandolier pouch, silver gorgets around his throat, and three white ostrich plumes adorning his turban.

67
00:10:52.960 --> 00:11:06.720
[George] Fearing that Seminole allies would attempt a rescue raid on Fort Marion, the army transferred Osciola and over 200 prisoners in late December 1837 to Fort Moultrie in Charleston Harbor, South Carolina.

68
00:11:06.720 --> 00:11:15.920
[George] But the harsh winter climate, combined with severe malaria and an acute throat infection known as Quincy, destroyed the young chief's failing health.

69
00:11:15.920 --> 00:11:21.440
[George] On the evening of January 30, 1838, realizing death was near.

70
00:11:21.440 --> 00:11:35.520
[George] Thirty-three-year-old Ossiola dressed himself in his full ceremonial war attire, shaved his face, painted his cheeks vermilion, and took the hands of his wives and children, passing away with serene dignity on his prison cot.

71
00:11:35.520 --> 00:11:46.960
[George] George Catlin's iconic oil portraits captured Ossiola's aristocratic dignity and fierce intelligence, creating an immortal visual record of an indigenous leader who refused to bend.

72
00:11:46.960 --> 00:11:52.240
[George] The treacherous capture and death of Ossiola did not end the Second Seminole War.

73
00:11:52.240 --> 00:11:56.480
[George] In fact, it inflamed Seminole resistance with renewed fury.

74
00:11:56.480 --> 00:12:11.920
[George] Under brilliant chiefs like Abiaka, known as Sam Jones, and Kokuchi, known as Wildcat, the Seminoles fought on for another four bloody years, refusing all offers of peace and retreating into the deepest southern fastnesses of the Everglades.

75
00:12:11.920 --> 00:12:25.760
[George] By 1842, having spent over $30 million dollars, more than the entire Louisiana Purchase, and lost over 1,500 regular soldiers to bullets and disease, the United States government simply gave up.

76
00:12:25.760 --> 00:12:36.840
[George] Without signing a formal peace treaty, the army withdrew its forces, leaving several hundred stubborn Seminoles living peacefully in the inaccessible hammocks of the Big Cypress and Everglades.

77
00:12:37.080 --> 00:12:46.040
[George] Those few hundred unconquered survivors became the ancestors of the modern Seminole tribe of Florida and the Micosuke tribe of Indians of Florida.

78
00:12:46.040 --> 00:13:01.080
[George] To this day, the Seminole tribe of Florida proudly carries the official title, the Unconquered Tribe, the only indigenous nation in North America that never signed a peace treaty surrendering their sovereignty or ancestral lands to the United States government.

79
00:13:01.080 --> 00:13:10.760
[George] Osceola's name is immortalized across America, in the name of Osceola County, Florida, in dozen of towns, schools, and naval ships.

80
00:13:10.760 --> 00:13:25.080
[George] His indomitable spirit stands as an eternal monument to the triumph of moral courage and love of homeland, proving that while an empire may conquer territory through deceit and iron, the human soul that fights for freedom can never be conquered.

81
00:13:25.080 --> 00:13:37.320
[George] The Seminole tribe of Florida's official status as the unconquered tribe stands as an eternal testament to the triumph of spirit, resilience, and unyielding love of native land.

82
00:13:37.320 --> 00:13:49.080
[Rebecca] The Seminole Resistance stands as a timeless testament to indigenous fortitude and love of native soil, keeping their sovereignty alive as the only unconquered tribe in North America.

83
00:13:49.080 --> 00:13:50.440
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

84
00:13:50.440 --> 00:13:56.280
[Rebecca] Join us next week as we journey across the Pacific to witness the rise of King Cayman.

